Chapter: PixelBufTest
  cites UI chapter PixelBuf

 The buffer is **8 wide and 4 tall**, never square. Every index here is
 `y * width + x`, so a version that reached for the height instead would
 still produce plausible pixels in a square buffer and wrong ones in this
 one.

 The last section pins the property the chapter exists for rather than
 any pixel: a write costs **no heap**. `alloc-bytes 0` advances the bump
 allocator by nothing and answers the current heap pointer, so the
 difference across a loop is the bytes that loop made permanent. The
 alternative in the tree, `Game chapter Rasterizer`'s `fb-set`, costs 24
 bytes per pixel because it returns a fresh record; at 1024x768 that is
 18.9 MB per full-screen pass with no collector under it. That is the
 whole reason there are two framebuffers, so it is measured here and not
 merely asserted in prose.

   `set` row alone, and the pixels land in the wrong rows. In a square
   buffer that sabotage is invisible, which is why this one is 8 by 4.
   The rect and line rows correctly do NOT move, because they write
   through their own loops rather than through `gop-buf-set`.
 - The blend's opaque shortcut moved from 240 to 200 changes the `a239`
   reading from 15724527 to 16777215 and nothing else, which is the
   239/240 pair doing the job it was chosen for.

 We say:

Section: Reading

  row : GopBuf, Integer -> Text
  row (gb) (y) = row-loop gb y 0 ""

  row-loop : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  row-loop (gb) (y) (x) (acc) =
    if x >= 8 then acc
    else let v = gop-buf-get gb x y
    in let c = if v == 0 then "." else if v == 1 then "1" else if v == 2 then "2" else "#"
    in row-loop gb y (x + 1) (acc & c)

  grid : GopBuf -> Text
  grid (gb) =
    let a = row gb 0
    in let b = row gb 1
    in let c = row gb 2
    in let d = row gb 3
    in a & "/" & b & "/" & c & "/" & d

Section: Construction and the single pixel

 A new buffer is filled, not merely reserved: `alloc-bytes` is a bare bump
 and hands back whatever the heap last held, so a constructor that skipped
 the fill would read as residue rather than as background.

  line-new : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in "new: " & grid gb

  line-set : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-set gb 3 1 3
    in let e = gop-buf-set gb 7 3 3
    in "set: " & grid gb

Section: Clipping

  line-clip : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let a = gop-buf-set gb -1 0 3
    in let b = gop-buf-set gb 0 -1 3
    in let c = gop-buf-set gb 8 0 3
    in let d = gop-buf-set gb 0 4 3
    in let e = gop-buf-set gb 0 0 3
    in "clip: " & grid gb

  line-clip-read : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-set gb 7 0 3
    in let a = show (gop-buf-get gb -1 1)
    in let b = show (gop-buf-get gb 8 0)
    in let c = show (gop-buf-get gb 7 0)
    in "clip read: left=" & a & " right=" & b & " inside=" & c

Section: Rect and line

  line-rect : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-rect gb 2 1 3 2 3
    in "rect: " & grid gb

  line-hline : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-hline gb -2 99 2 3
    in "hline: " & grid gb

  line-vline : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-vline gb 5 -2 2 3
    in "vline: " & grid gb

Section: Outline

  line-outline : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-outline gb 1 0 6 4 3
    in "outline: " & grid gb

Section: Blend

 Alpha runs 0 to 255. Black under white: at 0 the result is the
 background, at 128 the midpoint, at 239 nearly the foreground, and at
 240 the shortcut returns the foreground exactly. 239 and 240 are asked
 as a pair because they are the two sides of that boundary and nothing
 else in the range can show it.

  blended : Integer -> Text
  blended (alpha) =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let d = gop-buf-set gb 0 0 0
    in let e = gbf-blend-pixel gb 0 0 16777215 alpha
    in show (gop-buf-get gb 0 0)

  line-blend : Text =
    let a = blended 0
    in let b = blended 128
    in let c = blended 239
    in let d = blended 240
    in let e = blended 255
    in "blend: a0=" & a & " a128=" & b & " a239=" & c & " a240=" & d & " a255=" & e

Section: What a write costs

 The reason this chapter is in the foreword. A thousand writes must make
 no heap permanent, and the control loop establishes that a reading of
 zero is the allocator and not the instrument.

  heap-now : Integer
  heap-now = alloc-bytes 0

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (i) (n) = if i >= n then 0 else spin (i + 1) n

  write-loop : GopBuf, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  write-loop (gb) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let d = gop-buf-set gb 0 0 i
    in write-loop gb (i + 1) n

  line-cost : Text =
    let gb = gop-buf-new 8 4 0
    in let c0 = heap-now
    in let cd = spin 0 1000
    in let c1 = heap-now
    in let w0 = heap-now
    in let wd = write-loop gb 0 1000
    in let w1 = heap-now
    in "cost: control=" & show (c1 - c0) & " thousand-writes=" & show (w1 - w0)

Section: Body

  report-a : Text =
    line-new & "\n" & line-set & "\n" & line-clip & "\n" & line-clip-read

  report-b : Text =
    line-rect & "\n" & line-hline & "\n" & line-vline & "\n" & line-outline

  report-c : Text =
    line-blend & "\n" & line-cost

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni report-a
    print-line-uni report-b
    print-line-uni report-c
  end
